  "activity": {
    "id": "heart_anatomy_activity",
    "name": "Heart Anatomy and Blood Circulation",
    "description": "A comprehensive AR learning experience exploring the structural anatomy of the human heart, the four chambers and four valves, the major vessels entering and leaving the heart, and the dual-circuit path that blood takes through the pulmonary and systemic circulation. Students identify key structures, trace blood flow, understand wall thickness asymmetry between ventricles, and learn how valves prevent backflow during the cardiac cycle.",
    "workplace": "heart_anatomy_workspace",
    "start": "step_1_exterior_overview",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_exterior_overview",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Exterior Heart: Orientation and Major Vessels",
          "description": "You are viewing an intact human heart oriented anatomically with the apex pointing down and to the left. The heart is shown with the major vessels entering and leaving: the superior and inferior vena cava bringing deoxygenated blood from the body, the pulmonary trunk splitting into left and right pulmonary arteries carrying deoxygenated blood to the lungs, the pulmonary veins returning oxygenated blood from the lungs, and the aorta exiting to deliver oxygenated blood to the systemic circulation. Note that left and right are labeled from the patient's own perspective, not the viewer's. You can rotate the model to view it from any angle. Listen to the narration as it describes the gross anatomy and orientation."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "intro_station",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "intro_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "cutaway_heart_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "structural_tour_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "intro_station"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_structural_tour",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Cutaway View: Chambers, Valves, and Vessels",
          "description": "Now you see a cutaway heart with the anterior wall removed to expose the interior. This view reveals the four chambers: the right atrium and right ventricle on the right side (the patient's right), and the left atrium and left ventricle on the left side. Between the atria and ventricles lie the atrioventricular valves\u2014the tricuspid valve on the right side and the mitral (also called bicuspid) valve on the left side. Between the ventricles and their outflow arteries are the semilunar valves\u2014the pulmonary valve on the right and the aortic valve on the left. You will also see labels marking the superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, pulmonary arteries, pulmonary veins, and aorta. As each structure is highlighted, watch how it corresponds to landmarks on the intact heart displayed alongside. This dual view helps you map interior anatomy to the exterior surface."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "cutaway_heart_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "right_atrium_label",
              "poi": "right_atrium_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "cutaway_heart_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "right_ventricle_label",
              "poi": "right_ventricle_poi"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "cutaway_heart_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "color_coded_blood_animation"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "cutaway_heart_model",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "cutaway_heart_model",
            "duration": 2000
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_color_coded_blood_flow",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Dual Circuit: Deoxygenated and Oxygenated Blood",
          "description": "Blood flow is now displayed in color. Blue represents deoxygenated blood and red represents oxygenated blood. Watch as the flow streams through the heart continuously. The right side of the heart (right atrium and right ventricle) handles deoxygenated blood returning from the body via the superior and inferior vena cava. This deoxygenated blood is pumped to the lungs via the pulmonary arteries\u2014the pulmonary circuit. The left side of the heart (left atrium and left ventricle) receives oxygenated blood from the lungs via the pulmonary veins. This oxygenated blood is pumped to the entire body via the aorta\u2014the systemic circuit. You are seeing the two loops of circulation working in parallel. The pulmonary circuit exchanges oxygen at the lungs; the systemic circuit delivers oxygen to the body and receives deoxygenated blood back."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "cutaway_heart_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "color_coded_blood_animation"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "blood_flow_station",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "blood_cell_trace_animation"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "cutaway_heart_model",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "cutaway_heart_model"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_blood_cell_trace",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Following a Red Blood Cell: The Complete Path",
          "description": "A single large red blood cell begins its journey in the superior vena cava. Click the advance button to move the cell one step at a time through the entire circulation. At each step, the cell moves to the next chamber or vessel, a label names the structure it just entered, and narration describes the events\u2014how chambers contract to push the cell forward, which valves open and close, and what happens to the blood at each stage. The cell will traverse: right atrium \u2192 tricuspid valve \u2192 right ventricle \u2192 pulmonary valve \u2192 pulmonary trunk \u2192 pulmonary arteries \u2192 lung capillaries (where it changes from blue to red, picking up oxygen) \u2192 pulmonary veins \u2192 left atrium \u2192 mitral valve \u2192 left ventricle \u2192 aortic valve \u2192 aorta \u2192 systemic circulation. The cell then returns as deoxygenated blood through the systemic veins back to the superior vena cava, completing the loop. You can replay this trace as many times as you want to master the sequence."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "blood_flow_station",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "blood_cell_trace_animation"
            },
            {
              "target": "blood_flow_station",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "blood_cell_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "ventricle_cross_sections",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ventricle_thickness_comparison_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "blood_flow_station",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "blood_flow_station"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5_wall_thickness_asymmetry",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Why the Left Ventricle is Thicker: Pressure and Workload",
          "description": "Two cross-sections of the heart cut horizontally through the ventricles are displayed side by side. On the left, the right ventricle is highlighted, showing its thin myocardium\u2014approximately 3\u20135 mm thick in a healthy adult. On the right, the left ventricle is highlighted, with its substantially thicker myocardium\u2014approximately 10\u201315 mm thick. The thickness ratio is roughly three to one. Why this difference? The right ventricle pumps blood only to the lungs, a low-pressure circuit requiring relatively little muscular force. The left ventricle, however, pumps blood against the high resistance of the systemic circulation, delivering oxygenated blood throughout the entire body. This much greater pressure demand requires a much thicker myocardial wall. The cross-sections include scale markers so you can visually verify the thickness difference. You can rotate and compare the two sections directly."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "ventricle_cross_sections",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "right_ventricle_thin_label",
              "poi": "right_ventricle_cross_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "ventricle_cross_sections",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "left_ventricle_thick_label",
              "poi": "left_ventricle_cross_poi"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "cutaway_heart_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "valve_function_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "ventricle_cross_sections",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "ventricle_cross_sections",
            "duration": 2000
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
